{
  "rationale": "Evidence: iteration 4 is the best design so far (score 483.7). Oval finished 20 of 20 runs (progress 0.997, mean finish 1.99 s). S-bend finished 3 of 20 (progress 0.71, steering_oscillation 0.41). Slalom finished 0 of 20 (progress 0.32, all off track). Going from iteration 3 to 4 (ki 0.3 to 0.5, kd 0.5 to 0.6) lifted oval finishes from 9 to 20 and cut max_error to 0.017 m there. The integral is therefore cancelling the steady left/right motor-mismatch bias, and kd is damping the extra integral action. The remaining failures are on the tighter, alternating bends (s-bend and slalom), where oscillation is highest and progress is lowest. That points to an underdamped loop that responds too slowly to rapid direction changes.\n\nChanges (controller only, geometry unchanged so the effect can be read cleanly): (1) ki 0.5 to 0.7. The bias is still being removed (oval rms_error fell to 0.007), so more integral should tighten straights and reduce the offset carried into bends. The integral stays clamped at +/-2, which bounds windup. (2) kd 0.6 to 0.8. Oscillation on s-bend (0.41) is the largest signal of underdamping, so more derivative should help. (3) kp held at 4.0. Earlier, kp 5 with kd 0.3 overshot, and kp 4 with kd 0.5 to 0.6 is now stable on oval, so I am not raising kp until kd is shown to hold. (4) base_speed held at 1.3. Oval finishes in about 2.0 s, so there is time margin, but I do not want to trade off line-keeping on the tight tracks in the same iteration.\n\nExpected result: s-bend finishes rise above 3 of 20 and slalom progress rises above 0.32. Oval should stay at or near 20 of 20. Hypotheses: (a) rms_error keeps falling as ki rises, which confirms the bias is still being cancelled; (b) steering_oscillation on s-bend and slalom drops below 0.35 as kd rises, which confirms kd is damping the loop rather than adding lag. If oscillation rises instead, kd is adding noise and should come back down toward 0.6. Measurements wanted next: completion and completion_time per track; progress and max_error by track to see where the robot leaves the line on slalom; rms_error and line_loss_events to judge ki; steering_oscillation to judge kd; and controller_errors as a sanity check.",
